We demonstrate a numerical approach for simulating quantum systems under external driving forces via Metropolis algorithms. Specifically, we model a harmonic oscillator subjected to time-dependent driving. Although an exact analytical solution exists for this system, serving as a benchmark, our simulation yields results that align closely with the analytical expectations. These findings confirm the feasibility of employing Metropolis-based methods to simulate driven quantum systems. Additionally, we observe that incorporating imaginary time boundary conditions related to the system's driving frequency significantly enhances simulation accuracy. Furthermore, the study examines how the number of time slices and the associated time step influence precision, alongside an analysis of the simulations' overall accuracy.

