Building upon the research of [1], we develop a computational framework to extract coefficients for OPE block expansions of higher-spin operators within CFT$_{2}$. By ensuring the consistency of a nested structure of these coefficients, we identify a recurrence relation involving four distinct OPE block coefficients. This relation connects the coefficients of same-spin blocks on the left and right sectors when all blocks originate from the identical operator. Extending this approach, we establish a analogous recurrence relation for the combination of coefficients derived from two different operators, linking the (1,2) block on the left with the (1,2) block on the right to the coefficient of the (2,2) block formed by the product of the two operators. Leveraging this extended relation, we derive a closed-form expression for the (2,2) block coefficient when the operator corresponds to the stress-energy tensor $T$. Finally, we generalize this result to derive a closed form for the coefficient of the (n,n) block associated with the n-th order conserved current $J_{(n)}$. This generalization becomes possible through the introduction of a new decomposition technique for higher-spin blocks. We anticipate that this work contributes to the analysis of the conformal bootstrap method, particularly in terms bootstrap approaches, and supports the investigation of extended symmetries via bootstrap frameworks. Additionally, the extracted OPE block coefficients serve as a valuable resource for examining duality approaches to entanglement decompositions, such as E1-E1 and E2 blocks. Our findings further align with studies exploring the conservation of higher-spin blocks under conformal transformations and their associated symmetries, as well as research on the unitarity of these blocks and their symmetry properties. These coefficients also facilitate the construction of "negative mode" creations necessary for building entanglement bootstrap frameworks. Furthermore, the blocks themselves can be utilized in entanglement decompositions to explore equations of motion and consistency conditions of higher-spin blocks within this context. Regarding our notation, we adopt the convention that (n,m) denotes the (n,m) block of the operator $J_{(n)}$.

